Third-party data and models
===========================

The MIT licence in LICENSE covers the *code* in this repository. It does not
cover the corpus this project evaluates against, or the model weights it
downloads at runtime. Neither is redistributed here.

Corpus — Atas do Copom
----------------------
Published by the Banco Central do Brasil. The documents are fetched from the
institutional site (https://www.bcb.gov.br/publicacoes/atascopom); the same
material is published as a dataset on the BCB open-data portal,
https://dadosabertos.bcb.gov.br/dataset/atas-comunicados-copom, under the
**Open Data Commons Open Database License (ODbL) v1.0**.

"Public information" is not the same as "no conditions", so the conditions are
stated here rather than assumed. ODbL requires **attribution**, requires the
licence **notice to travel** with any redistributed database, and is
**share-alike**: a derived database that you distribute must be offered under
ODbL too.

What this repository actually distributes:

  * **Not the PDFs.** `data/raw/` is gitignored and no corpus document is
    committed. `uv run python -m ingest.pipeline --download 30` fetches them from
    BCB at run time, so the reader obtains the data from the source under the
    source's terms.
  * **`data/manifest.json`** — URL, title, reference date and SHA-256 per
    document. This is a small derived database, so it is offered under ODbL
    rather than under this repository's MIT licence, and BCB is credited above.
    The MIT licence in LICENSE covers the code; it does not and cannot relicense
    BCB's data.

Models, downloaded at runtime and never committed
-------------------------------------------------
  BAAI/bge-m3                       MIT                embeddings
  BAAI/bge-reranker-base            MIT                cross-encoder reranker
  spaCy (the library)               MIT                NLP runtime
  spaCy pt_core_news_sm             CC BY-SA 4.0       Portuguese model for Presidio
  Ollama: llama3.1                  Llama 3.1 Community License
  Ollama: qwen2.5:3b                Qwen Research License

Three of these are **not** MIT and the distinction matters. `pt_core_news_sm` is
CC BY-SA 4.0 — the *library* is MIT, the *model* is not, and it is the model that
is pinned by URL in `pyproject.toml`. The Llama 3.1 and Qwen licences carry
acceptable-use and naming conditions of their own. All apply to those artifacts,
not to this code, and every one is optional at run time: the `extractive` backend
needs no language model, and `guardrails/pii.py` degrades to a regex backend
without the spaCy model (loudly — see `docs/REPRODUCE.md` step 2b).

Synthetic data, labelled as such wherever it appears
----------------------------------------------------
  * The document ACL classification in `governance/acl.py`. Every ata is a
    public BACEN document; the five most recent stand in for a publication
    embargo. The enforcement is real and tested against live Qdrant — the
    policy is a fixture.
  * Every CPF, CNPJ, name, e-mail address and phone number in
    `eval/datasets/adversarial.jsonl`, `guardrails/` and `tests/`. The CPFs and
    CNPJs are checksum-valid test values chosen so the modulo-11 validators can
    be exercised; they belong to nobody. E-mail domains are reserved-for-example
    domains.
  * `tests/fixtures/gate_degraded.json`, which simulates a broken meeting
    resolver so the regression gate can be proven to fail. Its `_fixture_note`
    says DELIBERATELY DEGRADED.

Nothing in this repository presents synthetic data as measured data.
